          "question": "I get short of breath climbing two flights of stairs but I am completely fine at rest.",
          "answer": "Breathlessness on stairs while fine at rest is a common experience, and the safety check found no emergency pattern in this description. That is not a clearance. The check only covers a fixed list of symptoms.\n\n**Get emergency help right away** if you notice any of these, whether during exertion or at rest (red-flag guidance, \"Severe difficulty breathing\"):\n- Breathlessness at rest, or breathlessness that comes on suddenly\n- Being unable to speak in full sentences, or having to pause for breath between words\n- Blue or gray lips, tongue, face or fingertips\n- Chest pain together with breathlessness\n- Drowsiness, confusion or exhaustion while struggling to breathe\n\n**What the retrieved sources do not cover:** none of them explain what causes breathlessness on exertion or how it is evaluated. I can't tell you what might be behind your symptom from these sources.\n\n**A reasonable next step:** Tell a clinician about this pattern, especially if it is new for you or getting worse. Mention when it happens, how far you climb, and how long it takes to recover. Your clinician can decide what checks, if any, are needed.\n\n**General guidance you may find useful:** The physical activity guidance (Physical activity for high blood pressure) notes that short bouts of activity such as stairs count toward your weekly total. The breathing-during-exertion guidance (Staying active with COPD) describes pursed-lip breathing, where you breathe in through the nose and out slowly through lightly pursed lips, and paced breathing, where you breathe out during the hard part of a task. Resting with your forearms supported on a rail is also described as a recovery position. These are general techniques, not a substitute for a clinical assessment.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
